\\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\) and \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12} = \\sin (\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{12}) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\). Similarly, \\(\\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} = \\sin (\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{12}) = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\).\n\nThus, the sum can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}.\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2 \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4}.\n\\]\n\nWe know the values of \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) and \\(\\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\):\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 2 \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nCombine the constants:\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + 2 \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify further, we use the known values of \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\) and \\(\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\):\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n\\]\n\nSubstitute these values:\n\\[\n2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) + 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nSimplify each term:\n\\[\n2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\quad 2 \\left( \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\right) = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nCombine the fractions:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nSimplify the sum:\n\\[\n\\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2} + \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{2} + \\sqrt{2} = \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{2} + \\sqrt{2} = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}.\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To solve the given problem step by step, we need to compute the sum of the sines of specific angles:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12}.\n\\]\n\nFirst, simplify the angles:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12}.\n\\]\n\nWe recognize that \\(\\sin \\frac{9\\pi}{12} = \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\) and \\(\\sin \\frac{11\\pi}{12} = \\sin (\\pi - \\frac{\\pi}{12}) = \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\). Similarly, \\(\\sin \\frac{7\\pi}{12} = \\sin (\\pi - \\frac{5\\pi}{12}) = \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\).\n\nThus, the sum can be rewritten as:\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}.\n\\]\n\nCombine like terms:\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} + 2 \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4}.\n\\]\n\nWe know the values of \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) and \\(\\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4}\\):\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{3\\pi}{4} = \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus, the expression becomes:\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} + 2 \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n\\]\n\nCombine the constants:\n\\[\n2 \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} + 2 \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} + \\sqrt{2}.\n\\]\n\nTo simplify further, we use the known values of \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12}\\) and \\(\\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12}\\):\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4}, \\quad \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}